{"step_index":290,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:33:00Z","input_tokens":3018,"cache_read_tokens":118036,"output_tokens":2753,"thinking":"The quantitative pixel audit highlighted specific issues in `v6_03_crest_skybridge_moon.png` and `v6_04_banked_bowl_17deg.png`. These discrepancies emerged within the `dim = 1.0` Gwen Act 2 context.\n\n\nThe `dim = 1.0` (Act 2) images exhibit significantly higher `p05` values and prominent `roadWedge` percentages compared to Act 1 and Act 3.  The sky ramp in the `V5_SkyDome` code, specifically at line 720, and the high default strength value for `MAT_V6_HorizonMoonPortal` raise suspicion of a potential brightening effect in Act 2. This apparent washout effect needs further investigation to verify this hypothesis.\n\n\nThe AgX rendering pipeline desaturates extreme HDR values, potentially causing the observed white-out effect from the moon's high strength and bright horizon. The `MAT_V6_WetAsphalt` material exacerbates this by reflecting these over-bright values across the road surface.\n\nThe ambient world bounce, multiplied by the `_wbg.Strength`, lifts shadow values, resulting in pastel gray reflections on the wet road. The overall saturation across all six shots is lower than anticipated, requiring further investigation into the cause.\n\n\nThe combined effect of `World_V6_SpiderVerse` and `LGT_V6SkyFill` lifts diffuse irradiance, diluting the crosshatching effect. Adjusting these light sources significantly improves shadow depth and restores the intended color saturation. Tone mapping via AgX causes high emission strengths to wash out colors; calibrating `MAT_V6_HorizonMoonPortal` emission strength improves saturation and color depth.\n\n\nRevised color values for the SkyDome in Act 2, targeting Spider-Gwen's signature color contrast by shifting away from the blown-out white to retain the watercolor/neon teal-magenta identity. Reducing the strength of the neon materials to prevent AgX-related color distortion, achieving vivid raytraced streaks on the wet asphalt.\n\n\nThe EEVEE shadow buffer is overflowing. Doubling the buffer size and disabling shadows for distant skyline towers should resolve this. Also, converting preview renders to JPEG format will reduce file sizes.\n\nThe hero car's position within each shot requires verification; a short script will check image crops.\n\n\nExamined the wet asphalt material to understand the limited neon reflection. The original roughness ramp created an uneven distribution, with most of the road showing a higher roughness value than ideal for neon reflection. Adjusting the roughness ramp to create glassy rain puddles will increase the neon reflections. Also decreasing bump strength will avoid unnecessary scattering.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":"236","Path":"\"D:\\\\项目session\\\\blender-supercar\\\\pipeline\\\\v6_sv_env.py\"","StartLine":"210"}},{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":"775","Path":"\"D:\\\\项目session\\\\blender-supercar\\\\pipeline\\\\v6_sv_env.py\"","StartLine":"505"}}]}
